## Option C — `system_clock` + `year_month_day` only: simple, but not local-date safe

**Claim**: `year_month_day` can be constructed from a day-rounded `system_clock` time point, but this gives the date in the system-clock/sys-days timeline, not necessarily the current **local** civil date.

cppreference shows this pattern:

```cpp
const std::chrono::time_point now{std::chrono::system_clock::now()};
const std::chrono::year_month_day ymd{
    std::chrono::floor<std::chrono::days>(now)
};
```

Official doc: <https://en.cppreference.com/w/cpp/chrono/year_month_day>

LLVM libc++’s `year_month_day` supports direct construction from `sys_days` and `local_days`.

**Evidence** ([source](https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/blob/025b9a27cf74c16e1b1467aa8d7423abaac8363b/libcxx/include/__chrono/year_month_day.h#L46-L54)):

```cpp
public:
  year_month_day() = default;
  _LIBCPP_HIDE_FROM_ABI inline constexpr year_month_day(
      const chrono::year& __yval, const chrono::month& __mval, const chrono::day& __dval) noexcept
      : __y_{__yval}, __m_{__mval}, __d_{__dval} {}
  _LIBCPP_HIDE_FROM_ABI constexpr year_month_day(const year_month_day_last& __ymdl) noexcept;
  _LIBCPP_HIDE_FROM_ABI inline constexpr year_month_day(const sys_days& __sysd) noexcept
      : year_month_day(__from_days(__sysd.time_since_epoch())) {}
  _LIBCPP_HIDE_FROM_ABI inline explicit constexpr year_month_day(const local_days& __locd) noexcept
```

